            <ArticleTitle>Preterm spontaneous uterine rupture in a non-labouring grand multipara: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>586-589</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1701-2163(16)32892-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/S1701-2163(16)32892-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Uterine rupture can be a catastrophic obstetrical emergency. Many risk factors for uterine rupture have been identified, as well as a wide range of clinical presentations. In this case report, we describe the occurrence of fetal death and uterine rupture in a non-labouring, preterm patient.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 32-year-old woman, gravida 10, para 7, who had three previous lower segment Caesarean sections, presented with minor abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding. Fetal demise was confirmed on ultrasound examination. Delivery of the fetus by lower segment Caesarean section was planned, but at laparotomy a complete uterine rupture was found, with the amniotic sac intact in the mother's abdomen.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Uterine rupture can be a fatal event for the fetus, with the mother experiencing only minor symptoms. In managing patients with risk factors for uterine rupture, clinicians must always keep the possibility of rupture in mind. Abdominal trauma and intercourse may be added risk factors for uterine rupture.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
